{0 Rename refactoring}

Rename a note, heading, block ID, or explicit attribute anchor and update every
vault link or embed that resolves to it. Moving files or directories in the
editor updates the links the move would break.

{1 Input}

The [textDocument/rename] request supplies a cursor position and a non-empty
new name. The cursor may be on the definition or on a resolved link to it.

{1 Result}

The server returns a [WorkspaceEdit] containing edits for the definition and
all references. Edits replace only the semantic destination component: link
labels, wikilink aliases, embed markers, and other fragments are preserved.

Renaming a note includes an LSP file rename; the file remains in its existing
directory. Its links are updated as for a {{!file_operations}move}. Heading
definitions replace the heading text. Heading link fragments use the
new heading text; resolution subsequently uses its slug. Block and attribute
definitions replace only their identifier, without the [^] or [#] marker.

Results are grouped by document and ordered by byte position.

{1 Safety}

No edit is returned when the cursor has no resolved rename target, the new
name is empty, or an identifier contains characters outside letters, digits,
[-], and [_]. Note names may not contain path separators or be [.] or [..].
No edit is returned when the renamed note's new path is already indexed.

{1:file_operations File operations}

The server registers for [workspace/willRenameFiles] and
[workspace/didRenameFiles] on every file and folder.

For [willRenameFiles], each renamed file or folder is a move of every indexed
note and asset under it. The result is a [WorkspaceEdit] of text edits only;
the client moves the files itself, after applying it. The edits address the
files at their old paths.

A link is edited only if, after the move, it would no longer resolve to the
target it resolves to now. This covers:
{ul
  {- links into moved files whose written path no longer matches;}
  {- relative links out of moved files whose depth or parent changed;}
  {- links elsewhere that a moved file would take over, because it now
     matches the written path more closely than the current target.}}

Links that still resolve are left as written. The new destination keeps the
link's form: a relative destination stays relative; any other keeps as many
trailing path components as it had, adding more only until it resolves to its
target. The [.md] extension is kept only where it was written. Fragments,
aliases and labels are preserved.

A move is ignored for a project when its destination lies outside that
project. No edit is returned for a project in which two files would move to
one path or a destination is already indexed.

For [didRenameFiles], the index follows the moved files without rereading
them. A move across a project boundary instead rescans the projects involved.
